{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gr-potential",
  "connector": "〜られる",
  "gloss": "can (the potential form)",
  "title": "〜られる・〜える — can do",
  "pattern": "る-verbs: 〜る → 〜られる / others: 〜う → 〜える / する → できる / {来|く}る → {来|こ}られる",
  "explanation": "The potential form says you can do something. る-verbs swap る for られる: {食|た}べる → {食|た}べられる. Other verbs move the last sound to the え row and add る: {行|い}く → {行|い}ける, {飲|の}む → {飲|の}める, {話|はな}す → {話|はな}せる. する becomes できる, and {来|く}る becomes {来|こ}られる. What you can do usually takes が: {日本語|にほんご}が{話|はな}せます.",
  "natural": "In casual speech, る-verbs often drop the ら: {食|た}べれる, {来|こ}れる. It’s very common, though some people frown on it.",
  "stiff": "Don’t add できる to a verb: {行|い}くできる is wrong. Either {行|い}ける, or {行|い}くことができる, which is formal.",
  "examples": [
